arly in the  breeding pipeline as early as possible  we also work with our partners  to do simulations  to identify ways to leverage the tools  technologies and methodologies and  capacities that are being developed as  part of the ilc project to optimize  their breeding pipelines and maximize  performance in terms of varieties being  delivered to  farmers that meet their needs  to do this we're focusing really on  three core areas data digitization  analytics and the implementation of  continual improvement processes  while there are many definitions and  formal implementations of continual  improvement processes  within the breeding informatics area of  inquiry our focus is really just on the  core idea of continue improvement which  is the incremental improvement of a  process over time in many ways plant  breeding represents a continual  improvement process where we're trying  to make incremental increases in genetic  gain over time  so why the focus on continual  improvement well one because making a  series of very large and disruptive  changes to a breeding program decreases  probability of successful implementation  you can also put a very heavy strain on  programs  with limited or restricted capacity to  be able to make those types of changes  simultaneously  the second is that research and  innovation are not static we're  constantly developing new technologies  new methods new tools which in turn will  affect our breeding strategy as we try  and determine how to  leverage new tools and technologies to  maximize the efficiency of a breeding  program  also changes environmental conditions  changes in market conditions can lead to  different breeding objectives different  breeding focuses which in turn are going  to impact what types of enabling  capabilities need to be in place for a  breeding program  and this really represents an iterative  cycle that needs to be repeated  consistently year over year within a  breeding program so within the breeding  informatics aoi what we want to do is be  able to provide a lot of the the  software and analytical infrastructure  to enable programs to collect the types  of information  uh to have the systems in place to be  able to implement and effectively  implement a continuous or continual  improvement process within their  breeding program  the starting point for any engagement  with the breeding program is to first  really understand  what their current breeding strategy is  and what their current breeding  processes are now this could be a fairly  time consuming effort it revolves it  involves a lot of back and forth uh  between ill see  and breeders within the breeding  programs that we're working with but the  idea here is to try and document with as  much detail as possible exactly how  varieties are developed how they're  tested  how breeding objectives are being  defined what the focuses of a breeding  program are  this gives us a nice baseline to really  understand what the current state is  because that's really critical if we're  going to implement  improvement of a breeding strategy or  improvement of a breeding process we  first have to understand what the  current process is and for this we are  leveraging really some excellent tools  that were developed by the excellence in  breeding  platform  in particular we we use the excellence  in breeding pipeline manager  to work with reading programs to  identify really in detail what the  different steps of a program are  once we have a clear understanding of  what the current state of a breeding  program is what the current approaches  and strategies are  we then work with the program to start  to develop  simulation code to be able to develop  really an in silico version of the  breeding program  and to do this we leverage the alpha sim  r code  and we work with breeders to identify  various scenarios  we conduct analysis on historical data  to estimate parameters  and then we can work and go iteratively  back and forth where  we look at a scenario we test it we can  compare it to the current state we have  an iterative back and forth with the  breeders to identify ways in which we  might want to tweak a scenario  once we identify  different approaches to optimizing a  program that seem to have  high impact we can then move more onto a  consulting phase of the interaction and  breeding strategy where we're going to  work with our partners in the breeding  program to develop proof of concepts to  try and validate some of the findings  and also provide recommendations to the  programs on how they might go about  implementing some of these changes in  their breeding program and how we might  be able to track and monitor  the impact of these changes within a  program  one of the common scenarios that  breeding programs are interested in is  the implementation of genomic selection  and while in theory genomic selection  is often a far superior approach to  traditional breeding methodologies  it's also very complicated to implement  at scale in an effective way and  oftentimes programs don't necessarily  have a good idea of  where a good starting point is for the  implementation of genomic selection  in the breeding informatics area of  inquiry what we recommend is to take a  phased approach to the implementation of  genomic selection in the first phase  which we call informatics we're really  focused on implementing a lot of the  foundational capabilities that you need  to have in place to be able to  effectively utilize genomic selection  this is things like informatics  genotyping platforms imputation  platforms standard operating procedures  we recommend implementing genetic  quality control and pedigree  verification as a first step  and then  starting to do routine genotyping of all  material that goes into the pipeline now  a key element of genomic selection is  this ability to constantly refresh your  training set by routinely genotyping  everything that goes into a yield trial  we also work with programs to look at  you know what are some cost-effective  experimental designs that can enable  them to start routinely genotyping once  these foundational capabilities are in  place  we recommend moving on to optimization  of the actual variety testing a variety  development pipeline this would be  things like reducing time to  multi-environmental trials reducing the  number of years tested recycling earlier  within the vdp  once that's really in place and programs  are very comfortable with the idea of  genomic selection and they've validated  the value of implementing genomic  selection within their program  we start to work with programs to move  towards rapid cycling approaches which  in theory are the best but often in  practice some of the most difficult  approaches to work with when it comes to  the programs that we're working with  within the innovation lab for crop  improvement many of these programs  really are in phase one which is getting  in place the types of foundational  capabilities you need to implement  genomic selection and that's really  oftentimes our initial focus and our  engagement with our partners  our efforts to deploy enabling or  foundational  capabilities in technology really starts  with us  having a discussion with the eoc  partners to identify what are really the  key use cases and gaps  that are in place right now that need to  be addressed in order to move towards  more of a data-driven  approach to breeding  once we've had those conversations we  move towards deployment of appropriate  software systems  from there we identify functionality  gaps and deploy software systems and  finally our developers work across  multiple projects and with our partners  to write the types of software that's  needed to bridge these gaps and extend  functionality within the breeding  informatics  area of inquiry we really don't have the  capacity to build the types of large  software systems that need to be at the  core of any modern breeding program  thankfully many of these systems have  already been developed and so a lot of  our initial focus is just working with  the program to identify what the right  existing software solutions are and so  here on the slide i have some examples  of some of the major software systems  that are out there and that are in  common use within public sector breeding  programs and we can generally sort of  divide these up into systems that  support core breeding this would be bms  developed by ivp  breed base which is developed out of the  boyce thompson institute and the  enterprise breeding system that's being  developed by the cgir  in the genomics area there are two  major open source software systems that  are being widely adopted one is the gobi  project  negobi genomic data management system  and gig wall  finally you know field data collection  is a really important aspect of data  digitization we want to be able to have  data  flowing right from collection all the  way through to final results of analysis  without having a lot of manual steps  because that introduces or has the  potential to introduce errors and also  makes things much more complicated for  the breeders and so on the field data  collection side we work very closely  with pheno apps  and the effort in pino apps is really  being led by the phenomx area of inquiry  within ilse it should be noted that not  all of these systems can be so cleanly  defined into these broad categories  for instance breed based does have  genomic capabilities enterprise breeding  system  when complete will be deployed with the  gobi system on the back end handling the  genomic needs  where we come in with uh the breeding  informatics area of inquiry is the fact  that  for a lot of the types of use cases that  these programs need support for really  none of these systems individually can  fully support  and so there has to be a lot of work  to really  develop the piping between systems  to be able to fully support a workflow  from start to finish genomic selection  being a prime example of this you need  genomic data you need phenotypic data  as well as the analytics to be able to  effectively implement genomic selection  and while this has been widely  recognized as a really critical gap in  terms of what's available out there in  software for breeding programs it's been  traditionally very difficult  to address because these projects  the way in which they're organized  often makes it difficult to to really  you know develop the kind of  software that bridges these gaps between  systems and so  this is a gap that we recognize when  developing the project and we're fully  committed to being  really  focused on this aspect of providing  software solutions and new tools and  methodologies to our partners within  the elc project  within the elc project we try and be  agnostic in terms of what types of  software we work with our partners to  deploy and adopt that said we do have  some criteria that determines whether or  not we're willing to work with a certain  software project or a certain piece of  breeding software  first the software needs to be well  supported it needs to be well documented  and there need to be some accessible  resources for training and adoption the  second is the software needs to either  be broccoli compliant or committed to  working with us to implement broppy  for those that are not familiar with  broppy bropi stands for the breeding  application programming interface  broppy has defined has defined a  standardized  set of data model structures  that communicate the basic information  of plant breeding  it's a technical specification which  software developers can turn into code  which communicates using the standard  and the advantage of having these  standards in place is that when we're  trying to build functionality that  connects across multiple pieces of  software multiple software systems is we  can build it once and then it is easily  adaptable to different types of  configurations of software that any  given program might be deploying within  their breeding program  currently broppy provides specifications  for core breeding  for germplasm  for phenotyping and for genotyping  these represent  the types of capabilities that you need  to be able to support a large number of  breeding use cases but there are still  some gaps both in the phenomics space as  well as the analytics space and so  within elsi we're actively working with  the broppy project  to implement standards in some of these  additional domains to fully support  the types of workflows and the types of  use cases that we're trying to support  as part of the illc project  in terms of closing the gaps  what we really want to implement  as mentioned earlier is a fully  digitized  system in which  from the point of collection in the  field all the way through to results  that can be used to make decisions  either advancement recycling decisions  about potential changes to breeding  processes is completely digital  and completely supports the types of  work processes and breeding processes  that are being implemented within a  breeding program  we start by deploying existing systems  in this example we're looking at phenol  apps bms and gobi but we could easily  replace these  particular software systems with other  broppy compliance software and this type  of workflow would work the same  any breeding program that has deployed  these systems will quickly realize that  there are lots of gaps that are present  in terms of being able to fully support  modern breeding processes from end to  end from collection all the way to  decision support and so the software  developers within lc are primarily  focused on identifying these gaps  bropi to be able to map across systems  and then to build the type of code to  fill in these gaps and really provide  the types of end-to-end support you need  to fully optimize a breeding program and  to effectively implement continual  improvement processes  you know so what are we doing so we're  working on  building systems that that connect  between the various pieces of software  pheno apps is being deployed as part of  llc  it falls under the phenomx area of  inquiry but there's a long history of  phenoapps working with a lot of these  software systems and working with the  property community to implement these  types of connections that's not  necessarily the case for other pieces of  software for instance bms is a great  piece of breeding software but does not  provide the types of adequate support  needed for any type of genomic enabled  breeding process gobi on the other hand  is a fantastic software system for  managing genomic information for  implementing genomic specific breeding  processes but does not really have the  capabilities to manage core breeding  processes and so one effort that we're  really focused on right now is being  able to map broppy endpoints between the  bms and gobi system to enable these  types of uk use cases in a breeding  program another common gap is just being  able to run state-of-the-art analytics  that are needed to make the best  possible breeding decisions right now it  often times involves  you know pulling out excel files running  various types of software  to be able to effectively do this to  some extent you have to be a  biometrician or a quantitative  geneticist and have some coding  abilities so we're partnering with the  excellence in breeding project to build  you know a proper analytics engine that  can work directly with these systems to  enable breeders to be able to submit  requests for analyses have those run and  then have those results presented to  them in a way that makes it easy or  easier to make quality decisions about  the breeding program about advancement  decisions and about recycling  so where are we at currently with  the breeding informatics area of inquiry  well we have ongoing work with our  partner or our center of innovation in  west africa to document breeding  pipelines and identify simulation  scenarios and implement simulation code  we're focusing primarily on this coi on  the breeding strategy piece because they  are fairly advanced in terms of  deployment of breeding software  so they've already deployed bms to  manage their core breeding processes and  they're in the process of deploying  gigwa to manage their genomic  information  for our cois in west africa  they're really at the start of the  journey in terms of being able to  digitize their data and so we've been  working very closely with bms as a  partner  we've deployed the bms system and we've  completed training on the bms system  as mentioned we're currently mapping  broppy endpoints between genomic  management systems both gobi and digwa  and we have  initiated software development to begin  to start supporting genomic breeding use  cases  many of these programs aren't routinely  using genomic information yet but it is  on the horizon and we want to make sure  that these capabilities are in place  when they start doing routine genotyping  and finally significant progress has  been made in the development of  germplasm evaluation tools and this is  being done in collaboration with the  excellence in breeding we hope to see  the first release of this piece of  software  towards the end of the summer early fall  and we'll be continuing to enhance those  capabilities moving  forward with that i would just like to  thank everyone again for being here  and thanks again for the opportunity to  speak i look forward to the interactive  q a session  to be able to address any questions you  may have

